1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:02,100
I love parades.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:06,990
The Thanksgiving Day parade is always such great fun.

3
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:10,100
Look at that Superman balloon!

4
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:16,800
Wowee! Just floating along high above Central Park West.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,800
Don't you just love it?

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,700
Oh, and the bands and the music.

7
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:23,600
John Philip Sousa.

8
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,100
I love his music.

9
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:32,200
Da dada da da dada da da da da da da dada.

10
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:36,230
Oh, look at that float, Robbie.

11
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:38,500
Look at those funny-looking clowns.

12
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,600
It's for kids.

13
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:45,400
Maybe so, but parades always make me feel like a kid.

14
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,600
Remember when you and your dad and I

15
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,600
went to the Thanksgiving Day parade?

16
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:59,980
You were four or five years old, I think.

17
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:03,750
Hello, hello.... Oh, hi, Susan.

18
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:09,780
How are you?... Here's Mom.

19
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:11,900
Hello, Susan.

20
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,550
Yes, he missed a phone call from Alexandra.

21
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:18,000
Yes, I know, but he'll get over it.

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,200
Good. Then you'll be here about five?

23
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:22,000
Oh, fine.

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,100
I look forward to seeing you and Harry and Michelle.

25
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:27,890
Drive carefully.

26
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:30,040
Good-bye.

27
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:31,140
Taste Ellen's turkey dressing.

28
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:33,600
It's delicious.

29
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:34,600
I'm not surprised.

30
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,300
It's Grandma's recipe.

31
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,000
It's my favorite part of the meal.

32
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,100
What about my famous apple pie?

33
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:44,880
Dad, your apple pie is my favorite dessert.

34
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:46,100
How's the parade?

35
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:56,990
OK.

36
00:01:56,990 --> 00:01:59,000
Mom! Is it for me?

37
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
No, Robbie.

38
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,600
Who was it?

39
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,680
Wrong number.

40
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:06,800
Philip, would you join me in the kitchen, please?

41
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:07,500
It's getting late.

42
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,300
We have vegetables to prepare.

43
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:10,000
Can I help with anything?

44
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:10,800
No, honey.

45
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,600
You just relax with Grandpa.

46
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:15,990
I'll get you to help serve later.

47
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:18,900
Ellen reminds me so much of Grandma.

48
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:20,900
What time does the Michigan football game come on?

49
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:21,800
Four.

50
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,700
Philip!

51
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:27,370
I'll be back to see the game.

52
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:31,100
Grandpa, when did Dad graduate from Michigan?

53
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:33,500
Let me think.

54
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:36,600
He graduated from medical school in 1960

55
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:41,600
and from the University of Michigan in 1956.

56
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:43,500
Did you go to Michigan, too, Grandpa?

57
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,800
Yup. I graduated in 1937.

58
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:50,800
I've got to start thinking about college soon.
